WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Question 0027: a cake shared by an arithmetic sequence,
a junior-level question.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.133
Two parts: numbers first,
then the general case, with a single peak.

3
00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:15.400
A whole cake of size 1. People come in turn,
and each cuts a slice of what is left.

4
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:20.867
Person k takes the fraction a + (k − 1)d
of what is left: an arithmetic sequence.

5
00:00:21.667 --> 00:00:27.767
For example a = d = 0.2: the fractions 0.2, 0.4, 0.6
and 0.8, and the fifth, with 1, takes the rest.

6
00:00:27.767 --> 00:00:31.767
The fraction grows, but what is left shrinks.
Who gets the largest piece?

7
00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:37.767
Part A: here a = 0.01 and d = 0.1, so
the fractions are 0.01, 0.11, 0.21, 0.31 and so on.

8
00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:44.667
The first person takes 0.01 of the whole cake,
a very thin slice.

9
00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:51.533
The second takes 0.11 of what is left, 0.99,
and gets 0.1089: more than ten times as much.

10
00:00:53.433 --> 00:00:56.933
The third takes 0.21 of 0.8811,
and gets 0.1850.

11
00:00:58.833 --> 00:01:03.067
The fourth takes 0.31 of 0.6961,
and gets 0.2158. Up again.

12
00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:10.467
The fifth takes 0.41, but only of 0.4803,
and gets 0.1969: the first drop.

13
00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:15.733
From here the shares only fall: the fraction grows,
but there is almost nothing left to cut.

14
00:01:15.733 --> 00:01:20.833
The eleventh would take 1.01, so
he takes all the rest: only 0.0003.

15
00:01:20.833 --> 00:01:25.733
The peak: the fourth person, with 0.2158 of the cake,
about 21.6 percent.

16
00:01:26.533 --> 00:01:30.533
Why the fourth? Two opposite forces:
the fraction grows, and what is left shrinks.

17
00:01:30.533 --> 00:01:36.033
Let Rₖ be what is left before person k.
He takes pₖ of it, and the rest goes on.

18
00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:41.333
Compare each person with the next: in the ratio
what is left cancels, and only the fractions stay.

19
00:01:41.333 --> 00:01:46.800
The next gets more when the ratio is above 1.
Put pₖ₊₁ = pₖ + d and open the brackets.

20
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.900
A quadratic condition on pₖ alone,
without what is left of the cake.

21
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.233
The positive root of p² + 0.1p − 0.1
is the threshold T, about 0.2702.

22
00:01:56.233 --> 00:02:00.233
Below the threshold the next one gets more,
and above it he gets less.

23
00:02:01.033 --> 00:02:05.500
Now the fractions themselves, a bar per person,
and the dashed line is the threshold.

24
00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:10.233
0.01, 0.11 and 0.21 are below the threshold:
so the shares grow up to the fourth.

25
00:02:10.233 --> 00:02:15.733
0.31 is the first to reach it, and all after are above:
from the fourth on the shares only fall.

26
00:02:16.533 --> 00:02:21.633
And his share: before the fourth there was left
0.99 times 0.89 times 0.79 of the cake.

27
00:02:21.633 --> 00:02:26.233
He takes 0.31 of that, and gets 0.2158:
about 21.6 percent of the cake.

28
00:02:27.033 --> 00:02:31.933
In the page's box you add one person at a time,
and his slice is cut from the cake.

29
00:02:31.933 --> 00:02:36.833
Three more people: the fourth,
with the largest share so far.

30
00:02:36.833 --> 00:02:41.133
The fifth already gets less: the ratio
to the one before drops below 1.

31
00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:44.867
And all eleven: the peak is at the fourth,
as computed.

32
00:02:45.667 --> 00:02:49.700
Part B: now a and d are general,
with d at most one half.

33
00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:55.900
The same steps as in part A, without numbers:
the next gets more when d(1 − pₖ) > pₖ².

34
00:02:55.900 --> 00:03:01.300
Divide by 1 − pₖ, positive for everyone
but the last, and a function f appears.

35
00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:06.800
f increases: the numerator p² grows, 1 − p shrinks,
and both are positive. The derivative agrees.

36
00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:12.567
Here is the curve f(p), and the dashed line
is y = d, the 0.1 of part A.

37
00:03:12.567 --> 00:03:18.067
Each person is a point on the curve, at his pₖ.
The fractions grow, so the points climb.

38
00:03:18.067 --> 00:03:23.533
The first three are below the line: the shares grow.
The fourth is above it, and so is everyone after.

39
00:03:23.533 --> 00:03:29.000
Once the condition fails, it fails for good:
one peak, where the curve meets the line, at T.

40
00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:34.267
The crossing is the positive root of
p² + dp − d: the general threshold T.

41
00:03:34.267 --> 00:03:39.367
The largest share is the first person whose fraction
reaches the threshold, which gives a formula for k.

42
00:03:39.367 --> 00:03:43.900
A check with part A: 2.60 rounds up to 3,
plus 1: the fourth person.

43
00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:47.900
The last person takes only what is left,
but when d ≤ ½ it does not change the answer.

44
00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:52.500
And when is there a tie? When someone's fraction
equals the threshold exactly.

45
00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:57.533
Example: a = d = 1/12. The threshold is
exactly a quarter, which is exactly p₃ = 3/12.

46
00:03:57.533 --> 00:04:02.267
So the third and the fourth get exactly
the same share: 55/288 of the cake.

47
00:04:03.067 --> 00:04:07.067
And if d is above one half? Capping the last fraction
changes the picture: see the follow-up questions.

48
00:04:07.067 --> 00:04:12.567
In the box's proof step each person is a point
on the curve, and the peak is the first above the line.

49
00:04:12.567 --> 00:04:16.967
With d = 0.25 the threshold rises to 0.390,
and the peak moves to the third person.

50
00:04:17.767 --> 00:04:23.367
To sum up: compare each person with the next,
and the largest is the first to reach the threshold.

51
00:04:23.367 --> 00:04:29.267
Try other a and d in the box
on the page of question 0027 on the site.
